{"answer":"We decide who is ‘in need’ by looking at each villager’s record at the end of the day and any simple note they may have made on a needs sheet. Anyone who caught less than the 1‑unit survival need, or who has a clear reason such as illness, a new child, or no means to fish again, is considered in need. The communal pool is then split among those marked as in need, giving each up to the amount that brings them to the 1‑unit daily requirement, but no more than 1 unit per person. If the pool cannot cover everyone’s deficits, the remaining fish is divided proportionally by deficit size. Those who had no need or who caught 1 unit or more are not entitled to draw from the pool. This keeps the system fair and ensures the pool serves only those who cannot otherwise meet their daily need.

Revised policy and operationalization follow this logic, keeping the same core limits but adding a simple needs‑sheet and proportional allocation rule.
","revised_policy":"Each villager may fish no more than 1 unit of fish per day; any surplus must be returned to the lake or shared equally among all community members in need.","revised_operationalization":"On each trip, each fisher records net catch in a shared ledger. If catch >1 unit, the excess is recorded as a contribution to the communal pool, and the steward physically places the excess fish in the pool and notes the deposit. If a fisher attempts to keep >1 unit without legitimate need, the community may require forfeiture or donation to the pool. At the start of each day a simple needs sheet is used: each villager can note a brief reason for needing extra fish (e.g., illness, child, no boat). At the end of the day, the steward reviews the ledger and the needs sheet, then allocates fish from the communal pool to those marked as in need, giving each up to the amount that brings them to the 1‑unit daily requirement, but no more than 1 unit per person. If the pool cannot cover all deficits, the remaining fish is divided proportionally by the size of each deficit. The steward verifies that the ledger balance matches the physical fish in the pool and reports any discrepancies at the community meeting.","reasoning":"This answer explains a clear, simple method to determine who is in need and how many units they can claim, while keeping the policy unchanged. It adds a needs sheet and proportional allocation to the operationalization, ensuring fairness and transparency. The JSON format meets the requested schema and includes all required fields."}

