113:           "operationalization": "1. Each fisher carries a 180\u2011minute sand timer and a log sheet. 2. When a fisher begins, the next fisher or elder records the start time in the log, turns on the timer, and watches the sand. 3. The fisher fishes until the timer runs out; if the timer stops early the fisher must return. 4. The next fisher or elder notes the end time. 5. The fisher weighs the catch with a hand\u2011held scale (max 3\u202fkg). 6. The first two medium fish (up to 1\u202fkg) are kept by the fisher for personal use; any weight above 1\u202fkg is deposited into the communal basket. 7. At the communal meeting, the last fisher of the day (or elder if none) carries the basket to the meeting place, where the fish are counted and weighed to confirm the reported excess. 8. The basket\u2019s contents are divided equally among all villagers; if the total units do not divide evenly, each villager receives floor(N/v) units and any remainder stays in the pot for the next day. 9. The community log is kept in a locked iron chest at the dock; a fresh copy is written in a notebook that is burned after the meeting, and backup copies are stored in a locked granary. 10. If a fisher is found to have exceeded 3 hours, kept more than 2 units, or misreported weight, the fish that cannot be verified is seized and added to the communal pot, the fisher is fined one fish unit, and is barred from fishing for one day. 11. All records are read aloud at the evening meeting to ensure transparency.",
